<!-- page 10 -->

Instead of stepping to the total element (here represented by 2) in one big step, the passage is divided into two steps. (Note 0 and 1 cannot be taken as representing total elements.) This example is not very interesting because the direction of approximation is unique. We need an example with some choice. $\square$

<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`Token`).** The token set is a three-element type, standing for $\Delta=\{0,1,2\}$.

**Definition (`master`).** The master set is the full set of tokens.

**Definition (`twelve`).** The intermediate neighbourhood $\{1,2\}$.

**Definition (`two`).** The singleton neighbourhood $\{2\}$.

**Definition (`mem`).** A subset of the token set belongs to the family precisely when it equals the master set, $\{1,2\}$, or $\{2\}$.

**Theorem (`mem_iff`).** A set is a neighbourhood if and only if it equals $\Delta$, $\{1,2\}$, or $\{2\}$.

*Proof.* Immediate from the three-element family. ∎

**Theorem (`inter_eq`).** The intersection of any two neighbourhoods is again $\Delta$, $\{1,2\}$, or $\{2\}$.

*Proof.* Case analysis on the two factors, using absorption of the master and the nesting $\{2\}\subseteq\{1,2\}$. ∎

**Definition (`neighborhoodSystem`).** The neighbourhood system on the three-token carrier with this membership predicate and master set.

*Proof.* The master is nonempty and a neighbourhood; every neighbourhood lies in the master; the intersection of any two neighbourhoods is again a neighbourhood by the preceding classification. ∎

**Theorem (`example_1_3`).** There is a neighbourhood system on $\{0,1,2\}$ whose master set is the full token set and whose neighbourhoods are exactly $\{0,1,2\}$, $\{1,2\}$, and $\{2\}$.

*Proof.* Take the system just constructed; the master and membership claims are the definitions together with `mem_iff` (and the identification of the intermediate neighbourhood with $\{1,2\}$). ∎
